{
100: {skill_tested: "Identify a stated limitation of an early design.", question: "According to the first paragraph, why were embossed visible letters slow to read by touch?", option1: "The letters changed shape from one page to another.", option2: "A fingertip encountered only part of each large outline at a time.", option3: "The letters could be printed only with mechanical devices.", option4: "Readers were prohibited from checking a character twice.", correctAnswer: "A fingertip encountered only part of each large outline at a time.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage states that a finger meets only a small portion of the outline at once.<br><br>Step 2: Readers therefore had to move, remember, and check repeatedly to recognize a character.<br><br>Answer: A fingertip encountered only part of each large outline at a time.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Textual Details", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
101: {skill_tested: "Determine the contextual meaning of a design term.", question: "As used in the fourth paragraph, the word 'carry' most nearly means:", option1: "physically transport between schools.", option2: "press into the back of a sheet.", option3: "represent or communicate.", option4: "preserve an embossed outline.", correctAnswer: "represent or communicate.", explanation: "Step 1: The paragraph discusses how many meanings a limited set of dot patterns must express.<br><br>Step 2: In that context, what the system must 'carry' is information, so the word means represent or communicate.<br><br>Answer: represent or communicate.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Vocabulary in Context", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
102: {skill_tested: "Identify the central idea of a design history.", question: "Which statement best expresses the central idea of the passage?", option1: "Accessible writing improved when designers copied printed letters more accurately.", option2: "A large tactile character is always easier to identify than a compact one.", option3: "Specialized notation could expand only by assigning one unique cell to every symbol.", option4: "Tactile literacy developed through a sensory-appropriate design refined by users, tools, and shared conventions.", correctAnswer: "Tactile literacy developed through a sensory-appropriate design refined by users, tools, and shared conventions.", explanation: "Step 1: Compact cells respect how touch gathers information better than enlarged visible outlines do.<br><br>Step 2: Reader testing, production tools, standardization, and contextual rules turn that principle into a broad literacy system.<br><br>Answer: Tactile literacy developed through a sensory-appropriate design refined by users, tools, and shared conventions.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Medium", module: "Central Ideas and Themes", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
103: {skill_tested: "Explain why a familiar design can be poorly suited to another sense.", question: "The passage suggests that embossed visible letters appealed to many sighted teachers and printers mainly because the letters:", option1: "were familiar visually even though they were inefficient to perceive by touch.", option2: "could encode mathematics without any additional rules.", option3: "required fewer materials than compact dot cells.", option4: "had been selected through continuous testing by tactile readers.", correctAnswer: "were familiar visually even though they were inefficient to perceive by touch.", explanation: "Step 1: The outlines preserve shapes already recognized by sighted readers and therefore look reassuring.<br><br>Step 2: Touch must trace those large forms piece by piece, making them slow for their intended users.<br><br>Step 3: The appeal reflects the evaluators' sensory perspective rather than the readers' performance.<br><br>Answer: were familiar visually even though they were inefficient to perceive by touch.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
104: {skill_tested: "Analyze a tradeoff in compact encoding.", question: "The fourth paragraph indicates that selecting the number and spacing of dot positions required balancing:", option1: "visual familiarity against the cost of ink.", option2: "immediate tactile recognition against the range of meanings available.", option3: "school schedules against the speed of mechanical presses.", option4: "mathematical notation against ordinary prose.", correctAnswer: "immediate tactile recognition against the range of meanings available.", explanation: "Step 1: Too many positions permit many patterns but become difficult to perceive at once.<br><br>Step 2: Too few are easier to scan but cannot distinguish enough meanings.<br><br>Step 3: Refinement therefore balances perceptual compactness with expressive capacity.<br><br>Answer: immediate tactile recognition against the range of meanings available.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
105: {skill_tested: "Determine the function of user testing in a design history.", question: "The discussion of patterns that look distinct on a chart but feel ambiguous in text primarily serves to:", option1: "prove that every compact pattern is equally difficult to recognize.", option2: "explain why readers preferred raised visible letters.", option3: "show why visual inspection could not replace evidence from actual tactile reading.", option4: "argue that abbreviations should be removed from tactile writing.", correctAnswer: "show why visual inspection could not replace evidence from actual tactile reading.", explanation: "Step 1: A chart isolates patterns and allows them to be judged visually.<br><br>Step 2: Continuous tactile reading exposes confusions created by neighboring cells and movement.<br><br>Step 3: Reader performance supplies design evidence unavailable to sighted inspection alone.<br><br>Answer: show why visual inspection could not replace evidence from actual tactile reading.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Function and Structure", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
106: {skill_tested: "Explain how sequence expands a limited symbol inventory.", question: "According to the seventh paragraph, how can a compact system represent more meanings than it has individual cell patterns?", option1: "By enlarging each cell whenever a specialized symbol appears", option2: "By returning to embossed visible letters for technical material", option3: "By assigning every institution its own dot convention", option4: "By allowing one cell to change how a following cell is interpreted", correctAnswer: "By allowing one cell to change how a following cell is interpreted", explanation: "Step 1: The inventory of compact patterns is limited.<br><br>Step 2: Some cells act as signals that alter the meaning of the next cell.<br><br>Step 3: Meaning can therefore arise from ordered combinations rather than from one unique pattern per sign.<br><br>Answer: By allowing one cell to change how a following cell is interpreted", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Hard", module: "Sequence and Relationships", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
107: {skill_tested: "Synthesize why a usable system cannot be reduced to one invention.", question: "Why does the passage state that the invention is difficult to locate in a single object?", option1: "Practical literacy required separate developments in sensory design, writing, testing, production, notation, and agreement.", option2: "The earliest raised pages were destroyed before they could be examined.", option3: "Compact cells had no value until every specialized symbol received a unique pattern.", option4: "Readers and institutions adopted all competing conventions simultaneously.", correctAnswer: "Practical literacy required separate developments in sensory design, writing, testing, production, notation, and agreement.", explanation: "Step 1: Raised writing, compact cells, and broad standards appeared as distinct layers rather than one completed event.<br><br>Step 2: Users refined confusing patterns, tools enabled writing and copying, and sequential rules expanded expression.<br><br>Step 3: Because fluent, available literacy depends on the combined chain, no single raised object contains the entire invention.<br><br>Answer: Practical literacy required separate developments in sensory design, writing, testing, production, notation, and agreement.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Author's Purpose and Perspective", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"},
108: {skill_tested: "Derive a broader design principle from the passage.", question: "Which design decision would be most consistent with the passage's concluding principle?", option1: "Evaluate an audio interface chiefly by how closely its controls resemble printed punctuation.", option2: "Preserve the information a visual diagram conveys while reorganizing it for how listeners perceive sequence and emphasis.", option3: "Preserve the visible appearance of a chart even when users access it through sound.", option4: "Require users of a tactile system to learn the shapes of printed letters before reading.", correctAnswer: "Preserve the information a visual diagram conveys while reorganizing it for how listeners perceive sequence and emphasis.", explanation: "Step 1: The passage separates printed letters' linguistic function from their visual appearance.<br><br>Step 2: Compact tactile patterns preserve representation while adapting organization to touch.<br><br>Step 3: Reorganizing diagram information around auditory sequence applies the same principle to another sensory system without merely copying visual form.<br><br>Answer: Preserve the information a visual diagram conveys while reorganizing it for how listeners perceive sequence and emphasis.", practiceYear: 1, difficulty: "Very Hard", module: "Inference and Implication", imageSources: [], passage: "{{shared_passage}}"}
}
